extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Chart types | Column"
	- pagedescription = "Column charts in Tablechart: one bar per row, standing on the baseline, with the number above or inside it."

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Column
				p.lead One bar per row, standing on the baseline.

	+section("Column chart")
		p Each row of the table is a column. The number is shown above it.

		.card
			.demo
				figure(data-chart="column")
					figcaption
						b Apples picked
						br
						span thousands
					table
						tbody
							tr
								th Spring
								td 58
							tr
								th Summer
								td 72
							tr
								th Autumn
								td 70
							tr
								th Winter
								td 91

			+codeblock('HTML')
				figure(data-chart="column")
					figcaption
						b Apples picked
						br
						span thousands
					table
						tbody
							tr
								th Spring
								td 58
							+n
							// …one row per column

	+section("Numbers inside the columns")
		p If you want each number inside its column, at the top, add #[code data-chart-labels="inside"]. If you want it at the bottom of the column or in the middle, see #[a(href=url('/styling/text.html')) Text]. If a column is too short for its number, the number is shown above it.

		.card
			.demo
				figure(data-chart="column" data-chart-labels="inside")
					table
						tbody
							tr
								th Spring
								td 58
							tr
								th Summer
								td 72
							tr
								th Autumn
								td 70
							tr
								th Winter
								td 9

			+codeblock('HTML')
				figure(data-chart="column" data-chart-labels="inside")

	+section("Height")
		p The height of a chart is a share of its width, set with #[code data-chart-aspect]. The default is #[code 0.38].
		p On a narrow screen, a chart is never less high than #[code --tablechart-min-height]: #[code 200px] by default. Below that width, the height stays the same. It can be in #[code px], #[code em] or #[code rem], or #[code none] for no minimum. On a wide screen, #[code --tablechart-max-height] does the same at the top. See #[a(href=url('/styling/parts.html#height')) Height].

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="column" data-chart-aspect="0.6")
				table
					tbody
						tr
							th Spring
							td 58
						tr
							th Summer
							td 72
						tr
							th Autumn
							td 70

	+section("Below zero")
		p A column with a value below zero hangs down from the baseline, with its number under it. The baseline moves up to make room.

		+chart(null, '7-8,10')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption
					b Monthly result
					br
					span thousands of euros
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '12.6'], ['Feb', '28.7'], ['Mar', '-11.7'], ['Apr', '-36.3'], ['May', '19.8'], ['Jun', '-4.1']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		p With several value columns, each column of a group hangs down or stands up on its own.

		+chart
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption
					b Monthly result
					br
					span thousands of euros
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Month
							th 2024
							th 2025
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '12.6', '-2.6'], ['Feb', '28.7', '-12.2'], ['Mar', '-11.7', '12.0'], ['Apr', '-36.3', '26.8'], ['May', '19.8', '-6.9'], ['Jun', '-4.1', '7.5']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

	+section("Grouped columns")
		p If the table has more than one value column, each row is a group, with one column for each value column. The column headers are the names in the legend under the chart.

		+chart(null, '5')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption
					b Apples sold by variety
					br
					span thousands
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Variety
							th 2022
							th 2026
					tbody
						each row in [['Elstar', '118', '131', false], ['Granny Smith', '12', '45', 'granny'], ['Jonagold', '140', '135', false], ['Braeburn', '109', '121', false]]
							tr(class=row[3] || false)
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

		p Each column of a group is a bit lighter than the one before it. If you want to set a colour for each, use a #[a(href=url('/styling/colours.html')) palette]. A class on a row styles its whole group, as the Granny Smith does here:

		+style.
			.granny {
				--tablechart-bar-color: light-dark(#3a6413, #7fb03f);
			}

		p If the numbers are wider than their columns, they all get smaller together, down to 70% of their size. If they still do not fit, the chart has to omit all of them. If you hover over a group of columns (or tap it on a touch device), a tooltip shows all its numbers.

	+section("Stacked columns")
		p If the figure has #[code data-chart-stack], the values of a row are shown on top of each other, the first value column at the bottom. Values below zero are stacked down from the baseline. The total of the row is above the column.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="column" data-chart-stack)
				figcaption
					b Apples sold
					br
					span thousands
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Year
							th Farm shop
							th Market
					tbody
						each row in [['2023', '64', '38'], ['2024', '71', '45'], ['2025', '69', '57'], ['2026', '80', '62']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

		p A number is shown inside its part of the column if it fits. If it does not fit, the chart has to omit it. If you hover over the column (or tap it on a touch device), a tooltip shows all its numbers.
		p Below zero, the parts of a stack go down from the baseline. The total is the sum of all parts, above the column, or under it if the whole stack is below zero.

		+chart
			figure(data-chart="column" data-chart-stack)
				figcaption
					b Monthly result
					br
					span thousands of euros
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Month
							th Shop
							th Market
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '12', '-4'], ['Feb', '8', '-9'], ['Mar', '-6', '3'], ['Apr', '-14', '-7'], ['May', '4', '-2'], ['Jun', '10', '5']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

	+section("Columns and a line")
		p If you want a value column as a line over the columns, add #[code data-chart="line"] to its header. If a column has #[code data-axis="right"], it gets a value axis of its own, on the right. See #[a(href=url('/types/combo.html')) Combo].

	+section("Many columns")
		p With many columns, only the numbers of the largest values are shown, and the names under the chart get smaller or fewer. On a narrow screen, a column chart can be shown as a bar chart. See #[a(href=url('/features/many-rows.html')) Many rows].

	+section("Scale break")
		p If one column is much taller than the others, a break in the scale shows part of the scale smaller, so the other columns have room. See #[a(href=url('/features/scale-break.html')) Scale break].

		+chart(null, '10')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Elstar', '12'], ['Jonagold', '15'], ['Braeburn', '14'], ['Gala', '140']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="axis-break" data-from="20")

	+section("Axis and gridlines")
		p By default there is no value axis: the numbers on the columns show the values. If you want numbers along the axis, on the left, add #[code data-chart-axis]. If you want a light line at each of those numbers, add #[code data-chart-grid]. You can add one of them, or both. If you want to add them to every chart, set the #[code axis] and #[code grid] options to #[code true].

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="column" data-chart-axis data-chart-grid)
				figcaption: b Apples picked
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Spring', '58'], ['Summer', '72'], ['Autumn', '70'], ['Winter', '41']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		p If a chart has an axis or gridlines, its scale ends on a number of the axis. If you want to set the scale yourself, add #[code data-chart-scale], such as #[code data-chart-scale="0 100"]. If you want to change how the axis and gridlines look, see #[a(href=url('/styling/axes.html')) Axes and gridlines].

	+section("Next")
		p #[a(href=url('/features/annotations.html')) Annotations] add a bracket or a trend arrow to a column chart.
